==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{root|ang|ine-pro|*dʰeyǵʰ-}}
From {{m|ang|hlāf||bread}} + a second element representing {{der|ang|gem-pro|*daigijǭ||kneader}} (compare {{m|ang|dāg||dough}}, cf. {{m|ang|dæge}}). See also {{m|ang|hlāford}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|hlǣf-dīġe}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|f|head=hlǣfdīġe}}

# a [[lady]], the mistress of a household
# [[noblewoman]]; [[queen]]
# a form of courteous address; [[lady]]
# the [[Virgin Mary]]

====Usage notes====
* As feminine equivalents of {{m|ang|æþeling}}, {{m|ang|eorl}}, and {{m|ang|þeġn}} never developed, this was generally the title given to every noblewoman from the rank of [[baroness]] to [[princess]].

